l Chemotherapy Efficacy in NSCLC | ClinicalTrials.gov (https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT06346977?aggFilters=status%3Anot+rec+ava&cond=Diagnosis&rank=5&viewType=Table)\nciteturn2search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.6 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; ClinicalTrials.gov ID NCT06346977 ... Non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), occupying a disquieting position as the second most prevalent and deadliest neoplasm worldwide, afflicts an estimated 30% of its patients with intracranial metastatic spread. ... A Prospective Study on the Diagnosis of Leptomeningeal Metastasis and the Monitoring of Intrathecal Chemotherapy Efficacy in NSCLC Based on Proteomics\n\n## Diagnosis of Leptomeningeal Metastasis and the Monitoring of Intrathecal Chemotherapy Efficacy in NSCLC\n\nClinicalTrials.gov ID NCT06346977\n\nSponsor Union Hospital, Tongji Medical College, Huazhong University of Science and Technology\n\nInformation provided by Xiaorong Dong, Union Hospital, Tongji Medical College, Huazhong University of Science and Technology (Responsible Party)\n\nLast Update Posted 2024-04-04\n\nUnknown status \n\nLast verified\n\n2024-04 by Xiaorong Dong, Union Hospital, Tongji Medical College, Huazhong University of Science and Technology\n\nLast known status was\n\nNot yet recruiting\n\n## Study Overview\n\nBrief Summary\n\nNon-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), occupying a disquieting position as the second most prevalent and deadliest neoplasm worldwide, afflicts an estimated 30% of its patients with intracranial metastatic spread. Among these, leptomeningeal metastasis (LM) is an exceptionally surreptitious and perilous manifestation, often evading timely and accurate diagnosis. The clinical landscape is further complicated by the presence of patients who, due to various reasons, are unable to undergo lumbar puncture, a procedure crucial for the investigation of LM. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStudy Details | NCT00462982 | Sunitinib in 